\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Different squares on repeated copies}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take two copies of the positive line in \ref{fdip-017I}.
The direction is the identity, so the positive sign condition
allows both #{(z,w)\mapsto(\bar z,\bar w)} and
#{(z,w)\mapsto(-\bar w,\bar z)}. The first squares to the
identity and the second to its negative, as in \ref{fdip-017P}.
On three copies the cyclic permutation of the coordinates after
conjugation also fixes the direction. Its square is the square
of that permutation, which moves each coordinate. This square
is a nonscalar unitary even though every generator is fixed.}
